Fix a configuration and suppress its index for clarity. Write the half-score model as $y^H_{rj}=\mu^H_j+E_{rj}+\varepsilon^H_{rj}$, and centring across runs gives
\begin{equation}
d^H_{rj}=(E_{rj}-\bar E_{\cdot j})+(\varepsilon^H_{rj}-\bar\varepsilon^H_{\cdot j}).
\end{equation}
Assume that run-effect vectors are independent draws with covariance $\Sig$. Conditional on those effects, assume zero-mean item errors and zero covariance between $\varepsilon^A_{rj}$ and $\varepsilon^B_{sk}$ for the run and benchmark pairs in the calculation. Expanding $d^A_{rj}d^B_{rk}$ then removes its error cross terms in expectation, leaving
\begin{align}
\mathbb E[d^A_{rj}d^B_{rk}]
&=\mathbb E[(E_{rj}-\bar E_{\cdot j})(E_{rk}-\bar E_{\cdot k})]\\
&=(1-1/R)\Sigma_E(j,k).
\end{align}
Summing across runs and dividing by $R-1$ gives the unbiased covariance moment. We symmetrise the two half orderings for our matrix estimate,
\begin{equation}
\widehat\Sigma_E(j,k)=\frac{1}{2N(R-1)}\sum_{c,r}
\left(d^A_{crj}d^B_{crk}+d^A_{crk}d^B_{crj}\right).
\end{equation}
Symmetry follows from our construction of this estimate. However, positive semidefiniteness doesn't follow for a finite sample. A negative diagonal moment near zero doesn't by itself falsify the identity, although it prevents ordinary correlation normalisation for that trait.

For a diagonal additional covariance $D$ with nonnegative trace, the inflation ratio becomes
\begin{equation}
\Lam_D^2=\frac{\mathbf1^{\mathsf T}\Sig\mathbf1+\operatorname{tr}D}
{\operatorname{tr}\Sig+\operatorname{tr}D}. \end{equation}
This ratio lies between $\Lam^2$ and one when the denominators are positive, which explains the attenuation toward one under our restricted model. No general upper or lower bound follows once shared-item effects also contribute off-diagonal covariance. Bounding fixed-battery inflation by the item-general factor needs the same restriction.

