For each configuration and trait, we apply a uniform permutation $\pi_j$ of run labels to both halves together, after which the diagonal products sum to the same value and leave $U_c$ unchanged. Independent permutations across traits give zero expected off-diagonal products, because each centred deviation sums to zero over the runs. Conditional on a positive fixed pooled denominator, this implies
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E_\pi\left[\frac{\sum_cT_c^\pi}{\sum_cU_c}\right]=1.
\end{equation}
This expectation for the squared ratio doesn't imply that $\mathbb E_\pi[\widehat\Lam^\pi]=1$. Concavity of the square root gives an expectation no larger than one whenever all permuted ratios are nonnegative, with strict inequality for a nonconstant ratio. For example, equally likely ratios of 0.5 and 1.5 average to one, and their square roots average to about 0.966.

