\paragraph{Scoring-scale interventions}
\label{app:scale}
We transformed the saved margins item by item and recomputed each estimate. Each recomputation holds the items, runs, split, and interval routines fixed throughout. For benchmark $j$, $s_j$ is the pooled standard deviation of its per-item margins, ranging from 0.087 for WinoGrande to 0.468 for CommonsenseQA. \suptab{tab:scale} reports the resulting estimates, and a tenfold rescaling of every margin leaves the estimate unchanged to nine decimal places, which checks the construction.

\end{table}
\subsection{Reliability by trait}
The item halves contain 18,840 and 18,842 items across the battery, split within each trait and recorded for reuse across runs. The planning document proposed estimating reliability on eight screening recipes before inspecting seventeen estimation recipes, and we didn't implement that separation. Table~\ref{tab:original3} instead uses all 25 recipes, and our reliability checks and the decisions based on them remain exploratory.

We observe that reliability varies widely across the battery traits, since two accuracy traits have negative reliability estimates and two others fall below 0.10. The battery mean is 0.324 on accuracy and 0.652 on margins, BoolQ reaches 0.970 and 0.993, and ARC-Easy margins reach 0.927. Recomputing the per-trait information ratios gives a median of 1.608, a minimum of 1.571, and a maximum of 1.732. Those three figures reproduce what the pipeline summary reported without releasing the ratios themselves. The battery-level $\rho_g$ recomputes to 0.961 on margins and 0.734 on accuracy, against planning values of 0.61 for margins and 0.49 for accuracy at $\Lambda^2=1.819$.
\subsection{Power calculations}
\suptab{tab:original4} reproduces the planning arithmetic for 85 configurations and seventeen recipe clusters, whereas our completed analysis uses 125 configurations and 25 clusters. The reported linearised standard errors on the $\Lambda$ scale are 0.045 for margins and 0.038 for accuracy. Planning values there were 0.0935 and 0.1232, and our corresponding variance-ratio standard errors of 0.112 and 0.081 belong to the $\Lambda^2$ scale instead. Those two figures aren't comparable with the planning column.

The analysis didn't execute the proposed responses for G3 and G5, and G3 combines asymmetry with negative variance estimates, although a negative moment estimate doesn't establish leakage. The records lack a usable source-document identifier for the proposed alternative split. We retain the margin analysis after the G5 failure with the simulator sensitivity disclosed, and that departs from the proposed response. The source gate table has seven rows, and transport and the auxiliary-run contrast supply G6 and G8.
\subsection{Reported planning predictions}
The planning document specifies eleven predictions with rejection conditions, and \suptab{tab:original6} lists each criterion with its outcome without treating the document as verified preregistration. The first prediction requires $\Lambda_M>1.349$ and $\Lambda_A>1.40$ with lower confidence limits above one, and prediction two requires $\Lambda_A\geq0.8\Lambda_M$. Predictions three and four bound the accuracy excess retained after gain adjustment, together with the N5 share of the margin excess and the N6 share of the full-set excess. Predictions five to seven concern underprediction by independence across size bands, transport to PolyPythias, and an external seed mechanism with a bootstrap lower limit above one. Prediction eight requires at least eight traits with information ratios between 1.4 and 3.0. Prediction nine requires a cross-format diagnostic above 1.2 whose lower confidence limit also exceeds one. Prediction ten requires a Frobenius distance above 0.5, and prediction eleven more than ten percent of same-size configuration pairs with $P_{\rm flip}>0.05$.
\subsection{Excluded populations}
MultiBERTs provides 25 seeds for one configuration \citep{sellam2022}, whereas the 100 BERT runs of \citet{mccoy2020} vary fine-tuning from one pretrained checkpoint. The Pythia size ladder itself lacks seed replicates \citep{biderman2023}. The OLMo-2 checkpoints considered here lack replicates at matched steps \citep{teamolmo2025}, and the signal-and-noise panel lacks per-item outputs, which limits its use to the external check. We exclude these populations from our analysis because they lack the matched replicates or the item outputs required here.

