The cross-half identity follows \citet{spearman1904}, and \citet{cronbach1951} motivates the 50 re-splits in N3, which change the margin estimate by a reported standard deviation of 0.002. The \citet{brown1910} and \citet{spearman1910} formula shares the form $1+(k-1)\rho$ with $\Lambda^2$, but its correlation concerns parallel items. The reliability means in Table~\ref{tab:original3} don't estimate our inflation. \citet{campbell1959} cross traits with methods, whereas no benchmark here appears in multiple formats, and content and scoring format therefore can't be separated. \citet{brennan2001} provides the variance-component framework for these distinctions, and \citet{hofmann2025} and \citet{kipnis2024} describe ability structures across models rather than within-configuration noise covariance. \citet{hedges2010} handle dependent effect sizes without a complete covariance matrix, and our estimate supplies that covariance.
\section{Supplementary results}
\label{app:supplementary}

Our original scorecard counted five failures, four passes, and two predictions that couldn't be tested as written. Predictions 9 and 11 came from a separate analysis of the per-run reductions rather than the original pipeline. Prediction 7 lacks its required lower confidence limit. Prediction 6 depends on a post hoc choice to compare excess inflation. \suptab{tab:original6} retains the numerical outcomes and states where the assessment differs from the proposed criterion.
\paragraph{Variation across model sizes}
Each size band contains 25 configurations, one per recipe, and its clusters are therefore singletons without within-band replication. Only the 530M interval excludes one on either score scale, as Table~\ref{tab:original7} reports. However, that margin exclusion disappears under the test-inversion interval reported below. The intervals don't support a ranking of model sizes. All five margin and four accuracy point estimates exceed one, so prediction 5 passes a point-estimate reading and fails the stricter interval reading of the original scorecard.

