\paragraph{Registered held-out test}\label{app:heldout}
Our protocol fixed the task list, the replacement rule, the statistic, the interval, the pass rule, and a scope rule before any production score existed, and it records every later change as a dated amendment. The OLMES harness at a pinned commit builds every prompt and continuation, MedMCQA contributes 1,000 items drawn under a fixed seed, and DROP and CoQA are each capped at 1,000 items. Halves come from the clustered split of the passage-aware check with a seed fixed in the protocol. Items that share a passage or a story never straddle halves. The CoQA story grouping gives 479 groups for its 1,000 items. Before production, the six pilot checkpoints of one recipe at 150M and 1B each matched their own release seed on 300 ARC-Easy items. The backup rule then read the pilot's three 1B runs, where LogiQA-en gave mean accuracy 0.269 against a bar of 0.270 and LSAT-LR gave 0.219 against 0.220, with chance at 0.25 and 0.20. Both of those tasks fell short of their bars. The rule replaced them in its listed order with DROP and CoQA as multiple choice, and it reads accuracy only. Each miss is smaller than the pilot's binomial standard error of about 0.010 over three runs and 651 or 510 items. The rule rather than the evidence therefore decided the swap, and we applied it as written. Before each run was scored, a spread of its items was checked against a one-sequence reference path to within 0.001 nats, with a slower agreeing path as the fallback. Pilot timing projected 66.0 session hours for all 375 runs and 54.9 for the 225 runs in scope, and that projection triggered the scope rule. The same budget led us to withdraw a six-task secondary analysis that we had registered earlier.

